  "worked_solution": "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\u222d}_{R}3x\\,dV={\u222c}_{D}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}3x\\,dy]dA.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{D}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}3x\\,dy]dA & ={\u222c}_{D}3x[y]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=4}\\,dA \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{D}3x(4-{x}^{2})\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate this double integral over the region $D$ in the $xz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063571/assets/bcac3bb634fa04d2ccd4c3897f7b1db891a0b21eacfa96579f672b812fd92c07.png)\n\n \n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{D}3x(4-{x}^{2})\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-{x}^{2}}3x(4-{x}^{2})\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3x(4-{x}^{2})[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-{x}^{2}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3x(4-{x}^{2})(4-{x}^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(-2x)(4-{x}^{2})^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{[\\frac{(4-{x}^{2})^{3}}{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{0-{4}^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(-\\frac{64}{3}) \\\\  & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$",
